DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Uta Ruett (SLAC)\, Adi Natan (SLAC)\, Apurva Mehta (
 SLAC)\, Dennis Nordlund (SLAC)\, Dionisio Doering (SLAC)\, Nicholas Strang
 e (SLAC)\, Ashley White (LBL)\, Dula Parkinson (LBL)\n\n\nSuperintelligenc
 e (SI)\, capable of reasoning and acting in computational and physical env
 ironments\, allows us to re-envision X-ray science at user facilities a de
 cade from now. It offers the freedom to rethink how scientific questions b
 ecome experiments and how experiments lead to discovery. We will explore w
 hat we could learn about matter by overcoming the constraints of today’s
  instruments\, workflows\, photon delivery\, and ways of accessing facilit
 ies. In this context\, we will consider how SI\, automation\, and advances
  in X-ray capabilities could open new scientific directions and reshape ho
 w facilities are designed\, operated\, and used.\nWe welcome ambitious ide
 as\, unexpected connections\, and challenges to current assumptions. The g
 oal is to look for compelling scientific possibilities that could be enabl
 ed in one or two decades and identify the capabilities and choices that co
 uld bring them within reach.\nPreliminary workshop discussion outline:\n\n
  Current barriers: What are the main causes of unsuccessful experiments?
  What would improve the reliability and quality of experiments?\nComplex 
 automation and autonomous control: What kind of scientific question can w
 e address\, when SI can act on the experimental floor in many capacities? 
 How can we accelerate success and progress faster?How can SI enable in sit
 u changes to setups that would otherwise require a newbeamtime request in 
 a later allocation cycle?How can we leverage autonomous co-optimization of
  the source\, sample manipulation\, detector\, and data handling for nove
 l experiments? What are new approaches we could address?\nScientific reach
 :Which experiments are currently impossible\, and what prevents them?What 
 new opportunities arise when humans don’t need to enter the experimental
 areas?How can we move from experiments that require X-ray expertise to app
 roachesfocused on answering scientific questions?How can SI lower barriers
  for new users and new areas of science?\n\n\nhttps://indico.slac.stanford
 .edu/event/10814/
LOCATION:53/2-2002 - Berryessa (SLAC)
